{"name":"Hiroshima Aquamarine","hex":"#7fffd4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aquamarine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The bluish-green colour of the sea.","A transparent bluish-green, sometimes yellow-green, variety of beryl."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aquamarine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hiroshima","blocks":[{"text":"Hiroshima  is the capital of Hiroshima Prefecture in Japan. As of June 1, 2019, the city had an estimated population of 1,199,391. The gross domestic product (GDP) in Greater Hiroshima, Hiroshima Urban Employment Area, was US$61.3 billion as of 2010. Kazumi Matsui has been the city's mayor since April 2011. The Hiroshima metropolitan area is the second largest urban area in the Chugoku Region of Japan, following the Okayama metropolitan area."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Atomic_Bomb_Dome_and_Motoyaso_River%2C_Hiroshima%2C_Northwest_view_20190417_1.jpg/330px-Atomic_Bomb_Dome_and_Motoyaso_River%2C_Hiroshima%2C_Northwest_view_20190417_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiroshima","wikidata":"Q34664"},{"title":"Aquamarine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Aquamarine is a pale blue-green color between cyan and cerulean on the color wheel. It is named after the mineral aquamarine, a gemstone mainly found in granite rocks. The first recorded use of aquamarine as a color name in English was in 1598."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Aquamarine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquamarine_(color)","wikidata":"Q372973"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Hiroshima-Castle-1.jpg/500px-Hiroshima-Castle-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum